Checkpoint 13 owner validation
==============================

Status: implementation ready; owner recovery drill pending.

Safety boundary
---------------

Routine recovery restarts containers and reuses the PostgreSQL persistent
volume. It never restores an S3 dump automatically. A live dump restore is a
separate operator-approved command and is not required for the unquiet run.

1. Validate repository material
-------------------------------

Run as `codexuser` from the repository root:

    UV_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/webcam-uv-cache uv run pytest -q tests
    docker compose --env-file .env --profile application \
      --profile monitoring --profile jobs config --quiet
    bash -n deployment/systemd/pilot/run-maintenance-sequence
    bash -n deployment/benchmarks/run-checkpoint13-unquiet
    systemd-analyze verify deployment/systemd/pilot/*.service \
      deployment/systemd/pilot/*.timer
    git diff --check

Confirm the workers use `restart: unless-stopped`, have health checks, and
Windy enables batched freshness plus the bounded-minimum period strategy.

2. Install the production pilot units
-------------------------------------

Run from the administrator's own checkout:

    REPO_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
    sudo install -m 0644 "$REPO_ROOT"/deployment/systemd/pilot/*.service \
      "$REPO_ROOT"/deployment/systemd/pilot/*.timer \
      /etc/systemd/system/
    sudo systemctl disable --now webcam-discovery.timer \
      webcam-discovery-sequence.service 2>/dev/null || true
    sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    sudo systemd-analyze verify \
      /etc/systemd/system/webcam-stack.service \
      /etc/systemd/system/webcam-maintenance-sequence.service \
      /etc/systemd/system/webcam-maintenance.timer

The units intentionally run as `codexuser`; the administrator only installs
and controls them. The disabled discovery timer is the superseded two-schedule
checkpoint-12/early-checkpoint-13 unit; the cleanup-first timer replaces it.
Enable boot integration after the unquiet test is accepted:

    sudo systemctl enable webcam-stack.service
    sudo systemctl enable webcam-maintenance.timer

3. Validate a real backup without touching the live database
-------------------------------------------------------------

    just list-database-backups
    just validate-database-backup <canonical-object-key>
    just validate-database-restore <canonical-object-key>

Confirm length and SHA-256 match, `pg_restore --list` succeeds, the disposable
database contains `network`, `site`, and `source_stream`, and it is removed.

Do not run `just restore-database` here. If a live restore is later approved,
use the exact listed key. Confirm workers stop first, remain stopped after any
failure, and restart only after schema validation succeeds. Confirm
`estimated_source_stream_period` and other ingestion state are preserved.

4. Run the 90-minute unquiet exercise
-------------------------------------

Ensure no earlier benchmark workers or maintenance process is active, then:

    just checkpoint13-unquiet-test 90m

The command prints a unique screen session and `/tmp` log. It leaves
PostgreSQL, MQTT, and monitoring running, but stops ingestion at the end:

    T+00  build/start monitoring and all three full workers
    T+15  terminate Windy's PID 1 internally; require an increased Docker
          restart count and healthy status
    T+30  restart PostgreSQL; workers reconnect without S3 restoration
    T+45  start cleanup-first maintenance while ingestion remains active
    T+90  stop ingestion after any active maintenance sequence completes

Follow it with:

    screen -ls
    tail -f <printed-log-path>
    docker compose --env-file .env ps
    docker compose --env-file .env logs --since 10m \
      windy-worker fintraffic-worker skaping-worker postgres

Through the port-3000 tunnel, inspect all ingestion dashboards plus
Infrastructure health and Maintenance jobs. Confirm:

- every worker is initially healthy and produces completed epochs;
- Windy restarts after the injected kill, then resumes publication;
- PostgreSQL restart causes neither a worker restart loop nor dump restore;
- cleanup is attempted before every discovery and backup;
- a failed/timed-out discovery cannot suppress subsequent steps or backup;
- the maintenance lock prevents overlap and aggregate status is accurate;
- restore, maintenance, worker-health, and restart metrics are visible;
- resource use, throughput, epoch duration, and latency remain acceptable.

If maintenance outlives T+90, waiting for it is intentional: interrupting
cleanup or backup would invalidate the drill.

5. Reboot and explicit restore drills
-------------------------------------

After accepting the unquiet run, separately schedule an operator-supervised VM
reboot. Confirm the stack, worker health, monitoring, and maintenance timer
return. Keep any live restore separate and require explicit owner approval.

Email notification is deferred. Ignored local address/password files are only
placeholders and must not be committed or used without valid SMTP credentials.

Acceptance record
-----------------

Record: date, commit, backup key, isolated restore result, screen/log name,
restart times, maintenance outcomes, dashboard observations, reboot result,
and owner decision.
